/* data/learn-c/chem-12-14.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-14.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-14"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Living things are built from a few kinds of large molecules: carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and lipids. They form structures and take part in every life process. This chapter covers carbohydrates, proteins and nucleic acids.","w":["Biomolecules are large, but they are built from small units: sugars, amino acids and nucleotides.","Lipids are not polymers in the same sense as proteins and starch."],"x":["Living things are made of a few kinds of large molecules, called biomolecules. These are carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and lipids. They build the structure of cells and they take part in every process of life, such as getting energy, growing, and passing on the features of a living thing to its young.","This chapter looks at three of them. Carbohydrates are the main source of energy, and they also form the cell walls of plants. Proteins build tissues, and as enzymes they speed up the reactions of the body. Nucleic acids, DNA and RNA, store and pass on the genetic information.","All of these are polymers, made of many small units joined together. The units are monosaccharides in a carbohydrate, amino acids in a protein, and nucleotides in a nucleic acid. So a large part of the chapter is about the small units and the links that join them.","Study tip: make a small table of the unit, the link, and an example for each of the three groups, and learn it."],"e":"Compare: starch is a polymer of glucose units joined by glycosidic links. A protein is a polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds. DNA is a polymer of nucleotides."},"14.2":{"s":"Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones, or compounds that give them on hydrolysis. Monosaccharides (glucose, fructose) cannot be broken down further. Disaccharides (sucrose, maltose, lactose) give two units. Polysaccharides (starch, cellulose, glycogen) give many. Glucose exists mainly in ring forms. Sugars that reduce Tollens' or Fehling's reagent are reducing sugars; sucrose is not.","w":["Glucose is an aldohexose and fructose a ketohexose; both have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆.","Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar: both its reducing groups are used up in the glycosidic link."],"x":["Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones, or compounds that give them on hydrolysis. Monosaccharides, like glucose and fructose, cannot be hydrolysed to simpler sugars. Disaccharides, like sucrose, maltose and lactose, give two monosaccharide units. Polysaccharides, like starch, cellulose and glycogen, give many.","Glucose and fructose both have the formula C₆H₁₂O₆. Glucose is an aldohexose, with an aldehyde group at C-1. Fructose is a ketohexose, with a ketone group at C-2. In water, glucose is mostly in a ring form, with an α form and a β form that differ at C-1 (the anomers).","Tests: a reducing sugar has a free aldehyde, or a ring that can open to give one, and it reduces Tollens' reagent and Fehling's solution. Glucose, fructose, maltose and lactose are reducing. Sucrose is non-reducing, as both of its reducing groups are used in the glycosidic link. Glucose gives a pentaacetate with acetic anhydride, which shows five OH groups, and gluconic acid with bromine water, which shows an aldehyde group.","Sucrose gives glucose and fructose on hydrolysis (invert sugar). Maltose gives two glucose units, and lactose gives glucose and galactose. Starch (α-glucose), glycogen (branched, in animals) and cellulose (β-glucose) are the main polysaccharides."],"e":"Sucrose, 342 g/mol, with water gives glucose and fructose, 180 g/mol each. 171 g (0.5 mol) of sucrose gives 90 g of glucose and 90 g of fructose. The mass of the water used is 9 g."},"14.3":{"s":"Proteins are polymers of α-amino acids joined by peptide bonds (–CO–NH–). Amino acids exist as zwitterions. Protein structure has four levels: primary (the sequence), secondary (α-helix, β-sheet), tertiary (overall folding) and quaternary (several chains together). Heat or acid can unfold a protein (denaturation). Enzymes are proteins that act as specific catalysts.","w":["Amino acids are amphoteric: at the isoelectric point the zwitterion has no net charge."],"x":["Proteins are polymers of α-amino acids. An α-amino acid has an amino group and a carboxyl group on the same carbon, with a side chain R. The carboxyl group of one acid joins with the amino group of the next with the loss of water. The link, –CO–NH–, is the peptide bond. A chain of n residues has n − 1 peptide bonds.","Amino acids exist as zwitterions, with +NH₃ and COO⁻ groups, so they are amphoteric. At the isoelectric point the net charge is zero, and the solubility is the lowest. Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot make, so they must come from food.","Structure has four levels. The primary structure is the sequence of the amino acids. The secondary structure is the α-helix or the β-sheet, held by hydrogen bonds between C=O and N–H groups. The tertiary structure is the overall folding of one chain. The quaternary structure is how several chains are put together.","Heat, acid or other agents can destroy the secondary and tertiary structures. This is denaturation, as in a boiled egg. The peptide bonds are not broken, and the sequence stays. Enzymes are proteins that act as catalysts, each for a particular reaction. Common mistake: thinking that denaturation breaks the peptide bonds."],"e":"Glycine, 75 g/mol, forms the tripeptide Gly–Gly–Gly with two peptide bonds. Its molar mass is 3 × 75 − 2 × 18 = 189 g/mol. Gly–Ala and Ala–Gly are different dipeptides."},"14.4":{"s":"Nucleic acids, DNA and RNA, carry hereditary information. They are chains of nucleotides; each nucleotide is a sugar, a phosphate and a nitrogen base. DNA has deoxyribose and the bases A, G, C and T, arranged as a double helix with A–T and G–C pairs. RNA has ribose and uracil in place of thymine, and is usually single-stranded.","w":["DNA has deoxyribose and thymine; RNA has ribose and uracil.","Base pairs in DNA: A with T (two hydrogen bonds), G with C (three)."],"x":["Nucleic acids are DNA and RNA. They store the genetic information and pass it on, and they direct the making of proteins. They are long chains of nucleotides.","A nucleotide has three parts: a sugar, a phosphate group, and a nitrogen base. In DNA, the sugar is 2-deoxyribose, and the bases are adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). In RNA, the sugar is ribose, and the bases are A, G, C and uracil (U), in place of thymine. A sugar with a base is a nucleoside. A phosphate joins the nucleosides into a chain.","DNA is a double helix of two chains, with the bases inside. The bases pair: A with T by two hydrogen bonds, and G with C by three. So in DNA, the amount of A equals the amount of T, and the amount of G equals the amount of C. RNA is usually a single chain. There are three kinds: messenger RNA, transfer RNA and ribosomal RNA.","Common mistake: writing thymine in RNA, or the wrong number of hydrogen bonds. A–T has two, and G–C has three, so the G–C rich DNA is harder to separate."],"e":"A DNA sample has 28 percent adenine. Thymine is also 28 percent, which makes 56 percent for A and T. The remaining 44 percent is G and C, so guanine is 22 percent and cytosine is 22 percent."}};
